diff --git a/arch/dadao/Kconfig.debug b/arch/dadao/Kconfig.debug
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..041f325c0
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/dadao/Kconfig.debug
@@ -0,0 +1,441 @@
+# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0
+# DADAO debug options.
+#
+# These are boot-progress boundaries, not features: each one stops the kernel at
+# the point the corresponding milestone proves, so a later milestone turns it
+# off.  They default to n — an image that needs one states it (the K1 gate
+# configures its own image with `CONFIG_DADAO_K1_EARLY_STOP=y`), which is what
+# keeps "which boundary am I booting" an explicit property of the image instead
+# of an inherited .config.
+
+config DADAO_K1_EARLY_STOP
+	bool "Stop at the first line of start_kernel() (K1 boundary)"
+	default n
+	help
+	  K1 (m14-linux-003/005) only proves that the kernel *reaches*
+	  start_kernel(): the first statement of start_kernel() reports the
+	  boot info handoff and exits through semihosting SYS_EXIT(0) before
+	  any subsystem, clock, timer or interrupt is initialised.
+
+	  K2 turns this off and lets setup_arch() run (see
+	  DADAO_K2_EARLY_STOP).  The K1 gate keeps its own image: it sets the
+	  symbol with the explicit config-slice entry point.
+
+config DADAO_K2_EARLY_STOP
+	bool "Stop at the end of setup_arch() (K2 boundary)"
+	default n
+	help
+	  K2 (m14-linux-006) makes the supv synchronous-exception channel real:
+	  the entry captures the whole register file, the C half dispatches by
+	  cause and unrecoverable exceptions stop with the spec/Kaiwu §7.1
+	  monitor exit code.  This boundary reports the channel and exits
+	  through semihosting SYS_EXIT(0) once setup_arch() has run — before
+	  any clock, timer or interrupt controller exists.
+
+	  m14-linux-007 (timer) and m14-linux-008 (interrupt mask) move the
+	  boundary further: the default K2 image now stops at
+	  DADAO_K2_TIME_STOP below instead.
+
+config DADAO_K2_TIME_STOP
+	bool "Attach the timer and stop after the ticks (K2 time boundary)"
+	default n
+	help
+	  m14-linux-007 attaches the clock: at the end of setup_arch() the
+	  kernel reads cfx_time, installs the cfx_hart supv vector, opens the two
+	  interrupt mask axes, arms the single-hart timer, waits for the
+	  configured number of TIMER ticks (jiffies advances on each) and stops
+	  the machine through semihosting SYS_EXIT(0).  This is the K2 boundary
+	  the default image carries; the exception gate pins the earlier
+	  DADAO_K2_EARLY_STOP for its own images.
+
+config DADAO_K2_TIME_CASE
+	int "K2 time self-test case (0 = production, 1 = IMASK, 2/3 = one axis)"
+	default 0
+	range 0 3
+	depends on DADAO_K2_TIME_STOP
+	help
+	  Selects what the K2 time boundary exercises, so each probe image pins
+	  its own case instead of inheriting it:
+
+	    0  production: both mask axes open, the ticks, then the stopped-timer
+	       control (this is the image the later milestones boot);
+	    1  + the IMASK case first: a masked expiry only sets the pending bit
+	       and releasing IMASK delivers that event exactly once;
+	    2  the source axis (the kernel's IE row) stays closed — no tick may
+	       be delivered even though the global axis is open;
+	    3  the global axis stays closed — no tick may be delivered even
+	       though the source axis is open.
+
+	  Cases 2/3 exist to show that the two axes are independently required
+	  (contract-see §3/§6.4); they are test scaffolding and never part of a
+	  milestone image.
+
+config DADAO_K2_IRQ_TEST
+	bool "Run the K2 interrupt-enable knob self-test at the K2 boundary"
+	default n
+	depends on DADAO_K2_TIME_STOP
+	help
+	  Exercise the software IE knob (asm/irqflags.h) from inside the kernel
+	  and print the physical mask register back after every step (see
+	  kernel/k2_irq_test.c): the save/restore round trip, two nested saves,
+	  arch_irqs_disabled() against the live register, a masked window that
+	  freezes the tick count, and an event made pending inside a live frame
+	  that must only be delivered after `escape`.
+
+	  The boot ends after the cases.  This is test scaffolding (the K2 gate
+	  builds a probe image with it); it is not part of the image the later
+	  milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K2_TRAP_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K2 supv-exception self-test at the K2 boundary"
+	default n
+	depends on DADAO_K2_EARLY_STOP
+	help
+	  Exercise the exception channel from inside the kernel (see
+	  kernel/k2_selftest.c): a registered CFXMEM probe that is resumed with
+	  both escape offsets, the frame's register-file integrity, and the
+	  named termination of an unregistered fault.
+
+	  This is test scaffolding (the K2 gate builds a probe image with it);
+	  it is not part of the K2 image the later milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K2_SELFTEST_REENTRY
+	bool "K2 self-test ends with a fault inside the handler"
+	default n
+	depends on DADAO_K2_TRAP_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  The self-test's last case faults *inside* the handler, so the entry
+	  re-enters with the channel busy: the re-entrancy guard must report it
+	  as a kernel self-check failure instead of dispatching it.  The boot
+	  ends there, hence it is selected per image.
+
+config DADAO_K2_SELFTEST_CFXTRAP
+	bool "K2 self-test ends with an unregistered CFXTRAP"
+	default n
+	depends on DADAO_K2_TRAP_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  The last case executes `trap cfx_smon` with no probe registered, so the
+	  exception is fatal: spec/Kaiwu §7.1 puts CFXTRAP in the "other
+	  cfx" segment (0x80 | cfxHA), which the image then exits with.
+
+config DADAO_K2_SELFTEST_CFXESC
+	bool "K2 self-test ends with a CFXESC"
+	default n
+	depends on DADAO_K2_TRAP_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  The last case escapes to a cfx that is not the current one, i.e. the
+	  frame/target error of spec/DADAO-12 §5.1 (§7.1: same "other cfx"
+	  segment as CFXTRAP).
+
+config DADAO_K2_SELFTEST_UNKNOWN
+	bool "Corrupt the dispatched cause id (K2 unknown-cause probe)"
+	default n
+	depends on DADAO_K2_TRAP_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  `cfx_smon.excp_cause_id` is hardware-read-only, so the only way to
+	  exercise the dispatcher's "not in the 11-item table" path is to alter
+	  the value the C half receives.  This symbol adds that hook to
+	  kernel/traps.c; it exists only for the K2 gate's P4 probe image and
+	  must never be set in an image that boots anything.
+
+config DADAO_K4_STACK_SELFTEST
+	int "K4 user-stack growth self-test case (0 = off)"
+	default 0
+	range 0 6
+	help
+	  Exercise the page-fault channel's stack-growth branch (see
+	  mm/k4_stack_selftest.c): a missing-class fault below a VM_GROWSDOWN
+	  VMA extends the VMA, and the generic rules (`stack_guard_gap`,
+	  RLIMIT_STACK) decide how far.  This is m14-linux-019's S1-S6.
+
+	  Each value builds one image, because a case whose verdict is a named
+	  termination ends the boot:
+
+	    0  off — the only value a milestone image carries;
+	    1  S1 + S2 + S5: one page down, two pages down, and a permission
+	       fault that must not grow the stack; SYS_EXIT(0);
+	    3  S3: one step below the stack that would exceed RLIMIT_STACK —
+	       no growth, named termination;
+	    4  S4: a VMA without VM_GROWSDOWN — no growth, named termination;
+	    6  S6: growth that would enter stack_guard_gap — no growth, named
+	       termination (the neighbour is placed so the gap binds first).
+
+	  The fault is raised in supv mode (a kernel load/store to a user VA).
+	  A real user process pressing on its stack is the K5 closure's (s1/s6
+	  of check-linux-k5); a non-growing case still ends the boot by name here.
+
+	  This is test scaffolding (the K4 stack gate builds these probe
+	  images); it is not part of the image the later milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K4_COW_SELFTEST
+	int "K4 copy-on-write self-test case (0 = off)"
+	default 0
+	range 0 3
+	help
+	  Exercise the write half of a forked address space (see
+	  mm/k4_cow_selftest.c): a page the parent populated is shared read-only
+	  by the fork, and the first write by either side copies it.  This is
+	  m14-linux-018's W1-W5.
+
+	  Each value builds one image, because a case whose verdict is a named
+	  termination ends the boot:
+
+	    0  off — the only value a milestone image carries;
+	    1  W1 + W2 + W4 + W5: the child writes the shared page (its PTE is
+	       moved to a copy holding the old contents), the parent's page and
+	       contents stay put, the fork's second reference is dropped by the
+	       copy and the last one by the parent's unmap, and the parent's own
+	       write then resolves in place (its PTE was still read-only);
+	    3  W3: a write to a read-only page of a VM_SHARED mapping is *not*
+	       copy-on-write — it is a genuine permission violation, and the boot
+	       ends in the channel's named termination.
+
+	  This is test scaffolding (the K4 COW gate builds these probe images);
+	  it is not part of the image the later milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K4_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K4 process-model self-test at the K4 boundary"
+	default n
+	help
+	  Exercise the K4 process model from inside the kernel (see
+	  kernel/k4_selftest.c): create a child task through kernel_clone() and
+	  check that its address space is a full private copy of a parent's
+	  (different physical pages for populated pages, the same physical page
+	  for the shared zero page), that the child's saved context is the
+	  parent's with rd8 = 0, and that the page-fault channel's lookup answers
+	  through find_vma(current->mm) once the task has an mm.
+
+	  The boot ends after the self-test.  This is test scaffolding (the K4
+	  gate builds a probe image with it); it is not part of the image the
+	  later milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K4_SWITCH_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K4 context-switch self-test and continue to init"
+	default n
+	depends on !DADAO_K4_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  Exercise the context switch of m14-linux-017 from inside the kernel
+	  (see kernel/k4_switch_selftest.c): the register half of __switch_to()
+	  with a fabricated context, two kernel threads that take turns, the
+	  four-step switch_mm(), the minimal cfx_hart clockevent, and — because
+	  this image does not stop at the boundary — the real `kernel_init()`
+	  path into an initramfs `/init` (F3).
+
+	  Unlike DADAO_K4_SELFTEST this image *does* run other tasks, so it must
+	  not be combined with the single-task process-model probe.  The boot
+	  ends when the probe's syscall hook sees `exit_group` or a named failure.
+
+config DADAO_K4_KTHREAD_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K4 kernel-thread probe inside kernel_init (needs kthreadd)"
+	default n
+	depends on !DADAO_K4_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_SWITCH_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  Exercise the kernel-thread primitives of m14-linux-020 from inside
+	  `kernel_init`, after `kernel_init_freeable()` — the first point at
+	  which the boot has both kernel threads the probe needs (`kthreadd`
+	  itself, pid 2, created by `rest_init()` from the boot task, and the
+	  threads it creates on request; see kernel/k4_kthread_selftest.c) and
+	  the initcalls have run.
+
+	  It cannot run earlier than the initcalls: `kernel_thread()` forces
+	  CLONE_VM, so a clone from a task that has an mm sizes the futex hash
+	  through `futex_hash_allocate_default()`, whose `futex_hashmask` is
+	  only set by `futex_init()` (a core_initcall, i.e. inside
+	  `kernel_init_freeable()`); before that the bucket count clamps to 1
+	  and the allocation answers -EINVAL, failing the whole clone.
+
+	  Cases: K1 `kthreadd` exists, is pid 2, is a kernel thread with
+	  `mm == NULL` and real `fs`/`files` tables; K2 `kthread_create()` +
+	  `wake_up_process()` and `kthread_run()` start a body that runs,
+	  `kthread_stop()` makes `kthread_should_stop()` true (the body's
+	  return code comes back), and the task then leaves the run queue and
+	  its counter freezes; K3 `kernel_thread(fn, arg, CLONE_FS |
+	  CLONE_FILES)` shares the parent's `fs`/`files`, owns no user `mm`
+	  even when the parent has one, and leaves `rb3`/`thread.tp` at 0 —
+	  against a fork from the same task, which gets a private `mm` with a
+	  different ASID.
+
+	  The boot ends after the cases.  This is test scaffolding (the K4
+	  kernel-thread gate builds a probe image with it); it is not part of
+	  the image the later milestones boot.  User `clone()` (CLONE_VM /
+	  CLONE_THREAD / CLONE_SETTLS), TLS and futex are K5's.
+
+config DADAO_K5_SYSCALL_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K5 user-syscall probe from a late_initcall (ends the boot)"
+	default n
+	depends on !DADAO_K4_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_SWITCH_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_KTHREAD_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  Exercise the user syscall closure of m14-linux-023 (see
+	  kernel/k5_syscall_selftest.c): a user program of its own issues
+	  `trap cfx_umon, 0` with the Linux number in `rd15` and its arguments
+	  in the ABI's parameter registers, the entry dispatches the number
+	  through `sys_call_table`, and the results are judged from the frame
+	  the round trip left behind — `write()` returning its byte count and the
+	  bytes really landing in a ramfs file, `exit(0)` ending the task with
+	  status 0, the `rd8 = -errno` shape for an unsupported number and a bad
+	  argument (with `rd1` untouched), `rb3` (`rbtp`) unchanged across every
+	  round trip, and the cg5 frame fields plus the `immu18` parse.
+
+	  It runs from a `late_initcall`, i.e. inside `kernel_init` after the
+	  initcalls: only then is the initramfs unpacked and the VFS usable,
+	  which is what the `write` target needs (this image ships no
+	  `/dev/console`).  The boot ends after the cases.  This is test
+	  scaffolding (the K5 syscall gate builds a probe image with it); it is
+	  not part of the image the later milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K5_CLONE_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K5 user clone/thread probe from a late_initcall (ends the boot)"
+	default n
+	depends on !DADAO_K4_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_SWITCH_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_KTHREAD_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_SYSCALL_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  Exercise the user clone/thread surface of m14-linux-024 (see
+	  kernel/k5_clone_selftest.c): a user program issues `clone()` through the
+	  umon entry and the two tasks it creates are judged from the kernel side.
+
+	  `CLONE_VM`: the thread child's `mm` is the parent's, with the same
+	  `context.asid` and one more `mm_users`, while a `fork` from the same
+	  program gets an address space of its own with a different ASID.
+	  `CLONE_SETTLS`: the child's `rb3` (`rbtp`) is the `tls` argument and the
+	  parent's is untouched.  The child's `rd8 = 0` and the parent's `clone`
+	  return is the child's tid — the "dual return" itself stays in the user
+	  wrapper (contract-syscall §10).  `CLONE_THREAD`: one thread group
+	  (`tgid`, `group_leader`) and shared `fs`/`files`/`sighand`.  `rbtp`
+	  survives syscalls, `lr`…`sc` cannot cross one (SimRISC-12 §1), and
+	  `execve` clears `rbtp`.
+
+	  It runs from a `late_initcall` (the initramfs and the futex hash must
+	  exist) and ends the boot.  This is test scaffolding (the K5 clone gate
+	  builds a probe image with it); it is not part of the image the later
+	  milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K5_FUTEX_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K5 futex/atomic probe from a late_initcall (ends the boot)"
+	default n
+	depends on !DADAO_K4_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_SWITCH_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_KTHREAD_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_SYSCALL_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_CLONE_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  Exercise the futex surface and the user atomics of m14-linux-025
+	  (see kernel/k5_futex_selftest.c): one user program of its own issues
+	  the futex calls and the lr/sc pairs, and the probe judges what the
+	  kernel and the scheduler really saw.
+
+	  FUTEX_WAIT's "check the user word, then sleep" is atomic and a wake is
+	  never lost; a word that changed before the call makes the wait return
+	  -EAGAIN (the recheck under the hash-bucket lock); WAKE(n) wakes exactly
+	  n of several waiters; FUTEX_REQUEUE moves a waiter without waking it;
+	  WAIT_BITSET/WAKE_BITSET match on the bitset.  In user mode an `lr`…`sc`
+	  pair succeeds when nothing enters in between and fails after a syscall
+	  (spec/SimRISC-12 §1: an exception entry clears the reservation), and
+	  two threads exchanging the same word have exactly one winner.
+
+	  It runs from a `late_initcall` (the futex hash must exist) and ends the
+	  boot.  This is test scaffolding (the K5 futex gate builds a probe image
+	  with it); it is not part of the image the later milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_M45_ENTRY_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the entry/exit work-loop probe from a late_initcall (ends the boot)"
+	default n
+	depends on !DADAO_K4_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_SWITCH_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_KTHREAD_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_SYSCALL_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_CLONE_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_FUTEX_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  Exercise the generic entry/exit work loop of m14-linux-045 (see
+	  kernel/m45_entry_selftest.c).
+
+	  A user program runs a long loop (a user-only spin plus one `getpid` per
+	  outer iteration); the kernel wakes a worker thread and sets
+	  `TIF_NEED_RESCHED` on the looping task, and the verdict is that the
+	  worker ran while the loop still had iterations left — with no consumer
+	  for that bit the task returns to user mode every iteration and, on a
+	  single hart, the worker never runs.  A `SIGTERM` raised at the last
+	  iteration must end the task through `get_signal()`'s default action.
+	  The same run records `in_hardirq()`/`get_irq_regs()` inside every
+	  asynchronous delivery and `user_mode()` for both entry kinds.
+
+	  It runs from a `late_initcall` and ends the boot.  This is test
+	  scaffolding (the m14-linux-045 gate builds a probe image with it); it is
+	  not part of the image the later milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K5_SIGNAL_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K5 signal/exit probe from a late_initcall (ends the boot)"
+	default n
+	depends on !DADAO_K4_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_SWITCH_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_KTHREAD_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_SYSCALL_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_CLONE_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_FUTEX_SELFTEST && !DADAO_M45_ENTRY_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  Exercise signal delivery, the `rt_sigframe` of m14-linux-046 and the
+	  signal/exit surface of m14-linux-027 (see kernel/k5_signal_selftest.c).
+
+	  A user program installs a handler with `rt_sigaction` (SA_RESTORER, the
+	  only restorer a vDSO-less port has), raises SIGUSR1 and, once the
+	  handler has returned through `rt_sigreturn`, has the kernel compare the
+	  restored frame with the one it snapshotted at the raise: the whole
+	  register file must be bit-identical — the `ra` stack (RACNT and MRPTR
+	  included), the FCSR at its architectural value, every callee-saved
+	  register the handler deliberately scribbled over — with `ip` four bytes
+	  on, because the interrupted context resumes after the trap.  The handler
+	  itself checks the frame image it was handed against the registers the
+	  program set.  The same run raises SIGTERM with no handler (the default
+	  action must end the task) and repeats the round trip on an alternate
+	  stack (`sigaltstack` + SA_ONSTACK).
+
+	  m14-linux-027's behaviour cases run in the same image: a user access the
+	  page-fault channel cannot serve must become the process's SIGSEGV (not a
+	  boot-ending monitor code), `exit(0)` and a thread group leader's
+	  `exit_group(0)` must end the process/group with the right status, and
+	  `setrlimit(RLIMIT_STACK)` must be readable through `getrlimit`,
+	  inherited by a `fork` child and used as that child's stack-growth limit.
+
+	  It runs from a `late_initcall` and ends the boot.  This is test
+	  scaffolding (the m14-linux-046 gate builds a probe image with it); it is
+	  not part of the image the later milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K5_CLOCK_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K5 clocksource/clockevent probe from a late_initcall (ends the boot)"
+	default n
+	depends on !DADAO_K4_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_SWITCH_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_KTHREAD_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_SYSCALL_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_CLONE_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_FUTEX_SELFTEST && !DADAO_M45_ENTRY_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_SIGNAL_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  Exercise the generic time subsystem of m14-linux-026 (see
+	  kernel/k5_clock_selftest.c).
+
+	  The image boots with `time_init()` having registered `dadao_clocksource`
+	  over `cfx_time` and `dadao_clock_event_device` over `cfx_hart`'s compare
+	  register, and the probe reads the *registered* objects back:
+
+	  T1 read `cfx_time` twice through the clocksource and compare it with
+	  `sched_clock()` (same source, constant offset within tolerance);
+	  T2 stop the tick, program an 8-period deadline and then a 1-period one
+	  through `set_next_event()`, read `timer_cmp` back and measure where the
+	  delivery actually lands (the old deadline must be cancelled);
+	  T3 sample `jiffies_64`, the delivered-tick counter and
+	  `current->se.sum_exec_runtime` around N consecutive ticks: `do_timer(1)`
+	  and `sched_tick()` must both have run;
+	  T4 the clocksource is monotone and `ktime_get()` advances at sub-period
+	  resolution (i.e. timekeeping is no longer on the jiffies fallback).
+
+	  It runs from a `late_initcall` and ends the boot.  This is test
+	  scaffolding (the m14-linux-026 gate builds a probe image with it); it is
+	  not part of the image the later milestones boot.
+
+config DADAO_K5_PAGESZ_SELFTEST
+	bool "Run the K5 user 64 KiB ABI probe from a late_initcall (ends the boot)"
+	default n
+	depends on !DADAO_K4_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_SWITCH_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K4_KTHREAD_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_SYSCALL_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_CLONE_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_FUTEX_SELFTEST && !DADAO_M45_ENTRY_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_SIGNAL_SELFTEST && !DADAO_K5_CLOCK_SELFTEST
+	help
+	  Exercise the user-visible 64 KiB page ABI of m14-linux-028 (see
+	  kernel/k5_pagesz_selftest.c): the auxv the kernel writes for an
+	  `execve`d program, the ELF `PT_LOAD` mapping, `mmap`/`munmap`/
+	  `mprotect`, `brk` and the allocator's largest order.
+
+	  A1 the auxv on the stack `create_elf_tables()` built carries
+	  `AT_PAGESZ == PAGE_SIZE` (read back from the user stack, not from
+	  the kernel's copy) and `AT_ENTRY`/`AT_PHDR` agree with the ELF file
+	  and with the mapped image; A2 a non-`MAP_FIXED` `mmap` returns a
+	  page-aligned address, its length is rounded up to the page, `munmap`
+	  releases it and `mprotect` replaces the protection; A3 every `PT_LOAD`
+	  of the exec'd program is mapped page-aligned at its `p_vaddr` with
+	  the file's bytes in place (`p_align >= PAGE_SIZE`); A4 `brk(0)`
+	  reads a page-aligned break, growth maps whole pages, and an access
+	  one page past the break reaches the fault channel and ends the task
+	  in SIGSEGV; A5 `/proc/buddyinfo` has one column per buddy order
+	  (`MAX_PAGE_ORDER + 1`, i.e. the largest order is `ARCH_FORCE_MAX_ORDER`)
+	  and an allocation of exactly that order succeeds on this machine.
+
+	  It runs from a `late_initcall` and ends the boot.  This is test
+	  scaffolding (the m14-linux-028 gate builds a probe image with it); it is
+	  not part of the image the later milestones boot.
